---
description: >
  Alignment drift after the cold-start install refactor: stale scope path
  and identity-locked references to "aide_init" as the sole caller.
intent: >
  writeMethodology's scope still names the old path; the spec describes
  itself as a helper of "aide_init" rather than as a helper of the
  install service that aide_init and the CLI both consume.
misalignment:
  - spec-gap
---

## Issues

- [ ] `src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide` frontmatter
  `scope: src/tools/init/writeMethodology`
  Stale path — module moved to `src/service/install/writeMethodology/`.
  Traces to: physical move; ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` ->
  outcomes.desired[4] (folder tree as architecture).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide` Good examples:
  "A host project runs `aide_init` for the first time..."
  Identity drift — the example narrates the MCP tool path, not the
  install service. With two consumers (MCP tool and npx CLI) the
  example should narrate the service contract once and call out the
  consumer-specific shape, or distinguish the two narrations.
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` identity drift;
  ancestor `.aide/intent.aide` -> outcomes.desired[1] (two delivery
  channels).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

- [ ] `src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide` Strategy: "The
  parent spec's renderer invariants still govern..."
  References "the parent spec" generically; with the install service
  now sitting between this helper and `src/.aide`, the renderer
  invariants come from two ancestors (the install service and the
  trunk), not one. The spec should name which invariant comes from
  which ancestor or stop pinning to a single "parent".
  Traces to: parent `src/service/install/.aide` (misaligned — see its
  todo); ancestor `src/.aide` -> outcomes.desired[2] (no AIDE doctrine
  as string literals).
  Misalignment: spec-gap

## Retro

Architectural-shift drift. The "the tool runs aide_init" framing in the
Good examples is the highest-friction part of every moved spec — it
locks the narrative to a single consumer when the install service
should be consumer-neutral.
